    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=x^{2} - 2 x - 2$ at the point $(-1,1)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe intersection is $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$. Give exact values.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate the polynomial.\n\n$$\nf'(x)=2 x - 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_t=f'(-1)&=2 (-1) - 2=-4, \\\\m_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\\frac{1}{(-4)}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(-1,1)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-(1)&=\\frac{1}{4}(x-(-1)), \\\\y&=\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{5}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the $y$-axis, $x=0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right), \\\\y&=\\frac{5}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection is $\\left(0,\\frac{5}{4}\\right)$.",
